(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-statistics-causation-quiz"]={"title":"Statistics, Correlation and Causation Quiz 1 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A study has a statistical power of 0.90. What is its probability of a type II error?","choices":["0.10","0.90","0.05","0.01"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: power is 1 minus the type II error probability, so the probability of a type II error is 1 minus 0.90, or 0.10. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Ice cream sales and drowning deaths both rise in summer, and the link is statistically significant. What does this represent?","choices":["A sufficient cause","A dose-response relationship","A necessary cause","A non-causal association"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the relationship is statistically significant yet no causal relationship exists, because a shared factor such as summer weather drives both. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Koch's postulates for infectious disease illustrate which model of causation?","choices":["A web of causation","A single cause","Multiple interacting causes","An indirect chain of causes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the organism must be present in every case, isolated and grown in pure culture, cause a specific disease and be recovered from an inoculated animal. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A known mechanism explains how an exposure could injure tissue. Which criterion does this satisfy?","choices":["Consistency","Analogy","Biologic plausibility","Strength of association"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the relationship makes sense biologically, which strengthens the case that it is causal. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Poverty raises tuberculosis risk partly through crowded housing and malnutrition. What are those two factors called?","choices":["Intervening variables","Sufficient causes","Necessary causes","Confounders"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: in indirect causality one factor acts through intervening variables that are in turn directly causal. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What does the specificity criterion state?","choices":["One effect can have many causes","Larger exposure leads to more disease","One cause leads to one effect","Less exposure leads to less disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, though it is a demanding criterion, because a single exposure often produces several effects and a cause need not be specific to be real. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which finding argues against a causal relationship?","choices":["The disease appears before the exposure","Risk rises as exposure rises","Several studies replicate the association","A plausible biologic mechanism exists"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: an incorrect temporal sequence argues against cause, as do no dose-response, no reversibility, no specificity, no analogy or no biologic plausibility. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Lung cancer risk falls in people who stop smoking. Which criterion does this satisfy?","choices":["Dose-response","Reversibility","Temporality","Analogy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: decreased exposure is followed by less disease, which is the reversibility criterion and supports a causal relationship. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What does the null hypothesis state?","choices":["That there is an important difference","That there is no difference","That any difference is due to bias","That the sample is unrepresentative"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is usually the opposite of what the researchers hope to find; it is the hypothesis that statistical analysis sets out to test. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A study has a 20 percent chance of a type II error. What is its statistical power?","choices":["20 percent","95 percent","5 percent","80 percent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: power is 1 minus the type II error probability, so 1 minus 0.20 is 0.80. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A well-designed randomized trial finds no effect of an exposure on an outcome. What does that provide?","choices":["Strong evidence for a causal link","Weak evidence against a causal link","Strong evidence against a causal link","No evidence either way"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because a strong design combined with a finding of no effect gives strong evidence against cause, provided the trial had adequate power. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which design provides the weakest evidence for causation?","choices":["Cohort study","Case report","Non-randomized trial","Case-control study"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: it sits at the bottom of the hierarchy of designs, below case series and cross-sectional designs. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is statistical power?","choices":["The chance of detecting a real difference","The chance of reporting a false difference","The size of the difference between groups","The chance that the null hypothesis is true"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the probability that a study will find a statistically significant difference when the difference really exists. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which pairing best contrasts statistical and clinical significance?","choices":["Chance versus relevance to patients","Random error versus systematic error","Precision versus accuracy of measurement","Validity versus reliability of a test"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: statistical significance asks how likely chance is as an explanation, while clinical significance asks whether the difference matters to practice. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Before concluding that an association is causal, what must be excluded?","choices":["Dose-response, reversibility and specificity","Bias, chance and confounding","Temporality, strength and analogy","Precision, power and validity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, taken in that order: bias in selection or measurement, then chance, then confounding, and only if none explains it is a cause likely. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"How is the probability of an event estimated from counts?","choices":["Times it occurs divided by times it does not occur","Times it does not occur divided by times it can occur","Times it can occur divided by times it occurs","Times it occurs divided by times it can occur"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the result must lie between zero, meaning the event cannot occur, and one, meaning it will definitely occur. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which design gives the strongest evidence for causation?","choices":["Case-control study","Cross-sectional study","Randomized controlled trial","Case series"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, listed with systematic reviews at the top of the hierarchy of designs, well above cohort, case-control, cross-sectional and case series designs. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What does a confidence interval indicate about an estimate?","choices":["Its statistical precision","Its clinical importance","Its freedom from bias","Its statistical power"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: a narrow interval means a precise estimate and a wide interval a less precise one. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which 95 percent confidence interval for a difference in mean weight loss between two groups shows a statistically significant difference?","choices":["1.0 to 3.5 kilograms","-0.5 to 3.0 kilograms","-2.0 to 1.5 kilograms","-1.5 to 0.5 kilograms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: for a difference the value of no effect is zero, and this interval lies entirely above zero. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A study reports a relative risk of 1.2 with a 95 percent confidence interval of 0.8 to 1.8. What does that show?","choices":["A proven 20 percent increase in risk","A statistically significant protective effect","A precise estimate of harm","No statistically significant difference"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the interval contains 1, the value of no difference, so a true relative risk of 1 cannot be excluded. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Below what p value is a result generally accepted as statistically significant?","choices":["0.50","0.10","0.01","0.05"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, meaning a chance of about 1 in 20; a p value of 0.01 (1 in 100) is generally called highly significant and 0.001 (1 in 1000) very highly significant. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Several studies in different populations find the same association. Which criterion does this satisfy?","choices":["Biologic plausibility","Specificity","Temporality","Consistency"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: findings that are replicable in other studies make a causal relationship more likely. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A small trial finds a 30 percent lower death rate with a p value of 0.12. What is the best interpretation?","choices":["Statistically significant and clinically important","Possibly important but not statistically significant","Statistically significant but clinically trivial","Proof that the treatment has no effect"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the size of the difference could matter clinically, but a p value of 0.12 does not reach 0.05, and a small study may simply lack power. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"When no true difference exists, which conclusion is correct?","choices":["That the result is significant","That the null hypothesis is false","That the result is not significant","That the study has high power"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: with no true difference, a non-significant test result is right, while a significant result would be a type I error. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Two studies both estimate a relative risk of 0.6. Study A's 95 percent interval is 0.5 to 0.7 and Study B's is 0.2 to 1.8. Which statement is correct?","choices":["Study A's estimate is more precise","Study B's estimate is more precise","Both estimates are equally precise","Study B shows a significant reduction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: a narrower interval means greater statistical precision, and Study B's interval also contains 1, so it is not significant. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"In a clinic of 240 patients, 36 have the condition. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient has it?","choices":["0.85","0.18","0.15","6.7"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: 36 divided by 240 is 0.15, the number with the condition over the number of patients who could have had it. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How is statistical power calculated?","choices":["One minus the type I error probability","One minus the type II error probability","One minus the p value","The type II error probability itself"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: power equals 1 minus P beta, where P beta is the probability of a type II error, so power is high when a study is likely to detect true differences. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Lung cancer rates rise with the number of cigarettes smoked each day. Which criterion does this satisfy?","choices":["Reversibility","Dose-response","Specificity","Consistency"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: a larger exposure leads to a higher rate of disease. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A study makes 60 comparisons between groups, none reflecting a true difference. If significance is set at a p value of 0.05, about how many will appear significant by chance?","choices":["6","30","3","0"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: a p value of 0.05 corresponds to a chance of 1 in 20, and 60 divided by 20 is 3. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which differences tend to be more clinically significant?","choices":["Smaller differences","Differences from smaller samples","Differences with lower p values","Larger differences"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, although small differences can still be statistically significant, so size and p value must be judged separately. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"}]};
